/**
 * @brief Initializes and starts the IRC server
 * 
 * This function performs the three essential steps to start a TCP server:
 * 1. Creates a socket (endpoint for communication)
 * 2. Binds the socket to an address (IP + port)
 * 3. Sets the socket to listen for incoming connections
 * 
 * @details
 * Socket Creation (socket):
 * - Creates an unnamed socket with no IP address or port (UNBOUND state)
 * - Specifies AF_INET (IPv4) for address family
 * - Specifies SOCK_STREAM (TCP) for reliable, ordered, connection-oriented communication
 * - Returns a file descriptor (fd) that references the kernel's socket structure
 * 
 * Address Binding (bind):
 * - "Names" the socket by associating it with IP address and port
 * - Uses INADDR_ANY (0.0.0.0) to accept connections on all network interfaces
 * - Converts port to network byte order using htons()
 * - State changes from UNBOUND to BOUND
 * - Kernel adds entry to port table: port 6667 → listenFd_
 * 
 * Listen:
 * - Marks the socket as passive (accepting connections rather than initiating)
 * - Sets backlog queue size to 10 (max pending connections)
 * - State changes to LISTENING
 * - Kernel will now queue incoming SYN packets for accept()
 * 
 * @throws std::runtime_error if socket creation fails
 * @throws std::runtime_error if binding to port fails (e.g., port already in use)
 * @throws std::runtime_error if listen fails
 * 
 * @note Why socket() and bind() are separate steps:
 * - Servers MUST bind to specific port (clients connect to known ports)
 * - Clients do NOT need to bind (kernel auto-assigns random source port)
 * - Client flow: socket() → connect() (bind happens automatically)
 * - Server flow: socket() → bind() → listen() → accept()
 * 
 * @note Address format:
 * - INADDR_ANY (0.0.0.0) means "listen on ALL network interfaces"
 * - If machine has multiple IPs (127.0.0.1, 192.168.1.10, etc.),
 *   server accepts connections on any of them
 * - Clients connect using server's actual IP, not 0.0.0.0
 * Your Mac/Linux machine has:

 * 127.0.0.1        ← loopback (always present, for localhost)
 * 192.168.1.10     ← home wifi IP
 * 10.0.0.5         ← work VPN IP
 * 172.17.0.1       ← Docker network IP
 * 
 * @see accept() for accepting incoming client connections
 * @see socket(2), bind(2), listen(2) man pages for system call details
 */
